        <body>
            <div xml:id="d220-a2">

                <pb facs="0365" n="253" sameAs="frus:frus1910/pg253"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 2—Translation.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Prince of Ching to Chargé Fletcher.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >The Foreign Office</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1910-01-21"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">January 21,
                    1910</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Your Excellency</hi>: It is on record that the
                    viceroy of Manchuria and others have memorialized, asking permission to make a
                    foreign loan to build by installments a railway from Chinchow to Aigun. The
                    board of foreign affairs consulted with the boards of finance and communications
                    and it was agreed to sanction the proposal. A memorial stating specifically that
                    this sort of agreement should be drawn up in careful detail was sent in to the
                    Throne on the tenth day of the twelfth moon of the first year of Hsuan-t’ung
                    (Jan. 20, 1910)., and the vermilion rescript “Let it be as decided” was
                    reverently received. Besides instructing the viceroy concerned to act
                    accordingly, this dispatch is now sent for the information of your
                    excellency.</p>
                <p>A necessary dispatch.</p>
                <closer>[<hi rendition="#small-caps">seal of the wai-wu pu.</hi>]</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
